A||||16||GT Bastard||timing performance increase...||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||01-07-2000||06:42 PM||||Has anybody noticed a significant performance increase from advancing the timing slightly? I know that it made worlds of difference on my old '84 z28, but I haven't tried messing with my Fiero's timing.  I've heard that some engines respond better to timing advances than others.  Can anybody shed some light?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||01-07-2000||07:34 PM||||Generally the consensis here is that even a 1-2 degree timing error hurts a Fiero with a distributor. The ECM is pretty crabby about static timing.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||01-07-2000||08:58 PM||||thanks for the info ogre..||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||01-07-2000||10:37 PM||||The ol' small block Chevy V8's liked to run more timing advance, especially if you had a bigger cam and headers.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||01-07-2000||10:57 PM||||Back in the "olden days" we used to advance the timing until the motor wouldn't start then back just a touch. How's that for precision?||||reg||1
Z||000005||GT Bastard||01-07-2000||11:01 PM||||I know what you mean Phil.. My dad and I advanced the timing in my '84 Z until it started pinging, then we backed it off a hair.  That's the way the 'ol hot rodders do it I guess.  Of course, I ended up using a timing light anyhow.  It made a dramatic diff erence in performance.  That old 305 H.O. would literally burn rubber for 37ft with it's open-rear and 3.73 gears. It was nutz!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||01-07-2000||11:12 PM||||GTB- funny that you mention the 84 z-28 because I also had one with the 305 HO and the 3.73 open rear axle. I used to love manually shifting the trans at 6,000 to hear it smoke the tires when I shifted to second gear. I eventually put a 3.23 limited slip rear end from a Trans Am in it. Of all the cars I have had, the Z is one of my favorites. I just hated those leaky T-tops!||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||01-07-2000||11:47 PM||||Luckily, my t-tops didn't leak, but everyone used to ask me that!  They're fun fun cars, and I'm sorry I don't have mine anymore.  The tranny blew while I was cruizing down the highway... I ended up trading it in for my '94, but I miss it!||||reg||1
Z||000009||2FAST4U||01-08-2000||02:45 AM||||Phil,<BR>We used to use a paper match book cover to set point gap to, but then thats giving away our age.<P>I used to have 80 Z28 and 85 IROC, both had T-tops and both leaked, I think all t-tops leak after a while.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||01-08-2000||08:34 AM||||What else would you ever use to set the points?||||reg||1
Z||000011||GWMORGAN||01-08-2000||12:26 PM||||Feeler guage is used to set the points||||reg||1
Z||000012||Phil||01-08-2000||07:57 PM||||Feeler gages were what you put on your rocker panels or running boards to let you know how close you were to the curb.||||reg||1
Z||000013||2birds||01-09-2000||01:00 AM||||Phil,<BR>I'll bet you probably know how to adjust Model T coil boxes, too.  <P>I run 92 or 93 octane in my V-6 exclusively, and I can't get past 10 degrees base without audible detonation.  I tried splitfires once (waste of my money, IMHO) and couldn't do anything to stop the detonation, so I went back to Bosch platinums. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||88formula||01-09-2000||01:55 PM||||For all you guys who like to over advance your timing you should know that as you advance your timing your HC emissions goes higher and higher as well as NOX emissions. A good catalytic converter will hide this effect, but that's not good for your converter.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Phil||01-09-2000||02:12 PM||||Just let the boxes buzz||||reg||1
Z||000016||kidzwheelz||01-10-2000||05:26 PM||||   On the subject of timing, I have to say that for some reason, every car I own runs better with about 2 degrees of extra advance.<BR>I think its due to the altitude here in northern Utah. Haven't tried it yet in the Fiero because its not running just yet, but I'm sure I will try it. Don't know the technical reason that this works, but it does. Any of you fellas in Colorado notice this?||||reg||4
Z||000017||mrfiero||01-10-2000||06:00 PM||||I live in the Denver area, but haven't really messed with the timing.  Both Fieros I own are set at 10* BTC (factory setting).  <P>If you advance the timing shouldn't you also run a higher octane gas?  I had heard that you should to prevent "pinging".  <P>I may play with my Formula's timing to see if it helps or not.....I'll let you know what happens.||||reg||1
